-The preceding chapters validate the robot's mechanics: a force model for the braided pneumatic actuators (Chapter~\ref{ch:muscles}) and a corrected model of the joint torque those actuators deliver through the biomimetic knee (Chapter~\ref{ch:torque}). This chapter builds the other half of the neuromechanical system, the controller, together with the simulation pipeline required to develop it. The controller is a synthetic nervous system (SNS): a network of conductance-based neuron and synapse models whose organization follows the mammalian spinal cord \citep{rybak_modelling_2006, mccrea_organization_2008, deng_neuromechanical_2019}. Its parameters are not trained in the machine-learning sense; they are set from physiological data and then tuned against explicit verification tests, which is what keeps the circuits interpretable. Every neuron corresponds to an identifiable cell class, so the simulated system supports lesion, stimulation, and sensory-deletion experiments that cannot be performed on living subjects (Section~\ref{sec:bg_ethics}).
-
-Two definitions cover most of the neuroscience vocabulary used below. First, the locomotor controller is a two-level central pattern generator: a rhythm generator sets the timing of the step cycle, and a pattern-formation layer shapes that timing into phase-appropriate activation of antagonist muscle groups \citep{rybak_modelling_2006}. Each leg's rhythm generator is a pair of mutually inhibiting antagonist populations, one extensor and one flexor, so that only one is strongly active at a time. Second, in the non-spiking (graded) formulation used for most of this work, each neuron is electrically a nonlinear RC circuit: synaptic current charges a membrane capacitance through a leak conductance, and the resulting membrane voltage, tens of millivolts, is both the neuron's state and its output \citep{nourse_sns_2023}. Graded chemical synapses couple these units: a depolarized presynaptic cell opens a conductance in the postsynaptic membrane whose sign makes the connection excitatory or inhibitory. The sensory channels that close the loop are the muscle afferents, Ia signaling muscle length and velocity, Ib signaling muscle force, and group II signaling length, together with heel and toe contact sensors.
-
-Within neuromechanical simulation, the controller built here belongs to the biologically grounded SNS line, one of the three lines compared in Section~\ref{sec:bg_neuromech}; the reflex-based and learned alternatives \citep{geyer_musclereflex_2010, song_deep_2021} are treated there. The ancestry is direct: the two-level organization implemented throughout this chapter is the rat-hindlimb CPG of \citet{deng_neuromechanical_2019}, built on the full-muscle hindlimb analysis of \citet{young_analyzing_2019}, and the research community and tooling around the approach, including SNS-Toolbox \citep{nourse_sns_2023}, are surveyed in Section~\ref{sec:bg_sns}. Two-level CPG control has reached musculoskeletal macaques and muscle-driven robotic hind legs \citep{nishizaki_gait_2026, fukuoka_autonomous_2021}, though on non-humanoid platforms or with generic musculature. What this program adds is the combination the background identifies as unclaimed: an anatomically grounded two-level controller with Ia, Ib, group II, and contact feedback, driving BPA-actuated models characterized in Chapters~\ref{ch:methods}--\ref{ch:torque}.
-
-This chapter concerns tooling: its object is a verified virtual neuromechanical laboratory for the BPA-actuated robot. In this laboratory a controller can be built in any of several simulation engines, checked against its own re-implementations in the other engines and against the published behavior of the models it descends from, and then interrogated with the stimulus and deletion experiments that the locomotion literature applies to the biological circuits themselves. None of the infrastructure components is invented here: OpenSim, MuJoCo, MyoConverter, SNS-Toolbox, AnimatLab, Simulink and Simscape Multibody, SCONE, and the Sensory Afferent Database (SADb) of Chapter~\ref{ch:database} all existed before this program, and each is used for what it does best. What is new is the combination: the full-lower-body pipeline that converts the robot's OpenSim model to MuJoCo and couples it to an SNS-Toolbox spinal network; a derivative SNS block library for Simulink and Simscape Multibody; the Deng-lineage and Li-lineage controllers re-implemented across those programs and onto the converted models; and the SolidWorks robot assembly brought into Simscape Multibody. Re-running an existing model in the environment it was built in, as the preliminary walker was run in AnimatLab, reproduces the original publication and adds nothing; re-implementing the same controller in a second and third environment, and checking it against the first, is what makes the circuits portable objects rather than artifacts of one simulator, and the engine differences encountered on the way are part of the result rather than a nuisance. The demonstrations are therefore kept at the scale of a knee reflex or a replicated gait, because a tool that works at that scale needs no walking demonstration to be validated; the heavier evaluation series that do appear are reported for the questions they answer.
-
-Section~\ref{sec:prior_walker} opens with the preliminary AnimatLab walker results, whose limitations motivated the verification-first approach. Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods} is the methods section: the existing tools used, the new tools built on them, and the tuning and robustness methodology. Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_results} reports the demonstrations, the replications of published studies, and the stimulus and deletion experiments the tools enable; Section~\ref{sec:spiking_results} reports the spiking-neuron mirrors. Section~\ref{sec:sns_discussion} then discusses the training curriculum and its scoring, the integration of the BPA force model, the controller comparison on the robot models together with a precise statement of what walks and what does not, and the BPA-actuated robot, before closing with the new tools and the program they enable for Chapter~\ref{ch:futurework}.
+The preceding chapters establish how braided pneumatic actuator force and knee torque can be predicted. To study how neural control interacts with these mechanics, I also needed a simulation environment that connects anatomically organized spinal circuits to the robot's muscle routing and BPA force model. The existing tools reviewed in Chapter~\ref{ch:background} (Section~\ref{sec:bg_neuromech}) provide the neural, biomechanical, and physics components separately; using them for this robot required implemented interfaces and tests of the behavior preserved through conversion. The gap this chapter fills is that integration: no existing environment coupled an anatomically organized two-level controller, with Ia, Ib, group II, and contact feedback, to a BPA-actuated robot model with validated actuator forces.
+
+In this chapter I develop and evaluate those interfaces in MuJoCo with SNS-Toolbox and in a graphical Simulink workflow, and I verify them rather than assume them: a controller ported between environments cannot be presumed equivalent, so each port is checked against its source implementation and, where the lineage's archived models allow, against the published behavior of the models it descends from. The complementary roles of the environments reflect the complementary needs of the program, fast scripted closed-loop simulation for tuning and evaluation, graphical circuit construction for assembly and inspection, and CAD-derived mechanics for the physical assemblies; Section~\ref{sec:sns_new_tools} states which capability each environment was missing before this work. I also examine whether selected behaviors persist when graded neural components are replaced with spiking components. The contribution is the integration and its verification, with the distinction from existing Simulink libraries established in Section~\ref{sec:sns_new_tools} by direct comparison. The neural vocabulary, the two-level central pattern generator with rhythm-generator and pattern-formation layers, the graded neuron and synapse models, and the afferent channels, is defined in Sections 2.5--2.8 and used here without redefinition.
+
+The demonstrations establish specific component and closed-loop capabilities, and they identify failures of behavior transfer and limits of the assembled models with equal weight. Supported stepping and progression are distinguished from unsupported walking, which has not been demonstrated. Section~\ref{sec:prior_walker} records the AnimatLab walker whose hand-tuning motivated this verification-first procedure: manual tuning left the circuits' parameter sensitivity unmeasured, so the present procedure records performance under parameter and input perturbations instead. Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_methods} is the methods section, Section~\ref{sec:preliminary_sim_results} the results, Section~\ref{sec:spiking_results} the spiking mirrors, and Section~\ref{sec:sns_discussion} the discussion.
 
